  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Reducing Disaster Risk of Fragile and Mobile Communities in Timor-Leste</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Timor Leste is a country with medium disaster risk levels, vulnerable to climactic impacts on coastal areas, with a significant population residing in locations already impacted by coastal erosion and sea-level rise, leading to migration out of coastal areas. The rainfall pattern is projected to be more erratic and extreme, leading to increased soil erosion, frequent landslides, and local floodings. With the very high probability of extreme weather events due to the ongoing El Nino phenomenon, there is an urgent need to ensure the emergency preparedness of key disaster risk management actors to address the emerging gaps and changing country context. 
 
In response, IOM continues to support the Civil Protection Authority in improving its leadership capacity to prepare and respond to emergencies through inclusive and gender-responsive programmes on disaster risk management and improve the access of mobile and fragile community members to climate-resilient, safe and dignified evacuation centers during displacement.</narrative>
  </description>
